The del() method currently calls executeAsync() which has complex logic for handling cluster mode with MasterSlaveEntry mapping and CommandBatchService. This approach doesn't work with pipelines because it bypasses the normal command queuing mechanism. I should replace this with a direct write() call using LongCodec.INSTANCE and RedisCommands.DEL, passing all keys as a list. This will make del() participate in pipelines like other commands do. I'll also need to remove the unused checkExecution() and executeAsync() helper methods since they won't be needed anymore.

