[pacificporter/surf: Stateful programmatic web browsing in Go.]

# Support Compressed HTTP Response Encodings

The browser currently fails to handle HTTP responses that use compression encoding. Many web servers send content with gzip, deflate, or brotli encoding to reduce bandwidth, but the browser passes the response body directly to goquery without decompressing it first. This causes parsing failures when encountering compressed content.

The fix requires detecting the Content-Encoding header in HTTP responses and wrapping the response body with the appropriate decompression reader before parsing. We need to handle gzip, deflate, and brotli encodings. Additionally, proper resource cleanup is critical since compression readers must be closed separately from the underlying response body to avoid resource leaks.

