Added support for gzip, deflate, and brotli compressed HTTP responses by detecting the Content-Encoding header and wrapping the response body with appropriate decompression readers. The browser now reads the entire response body into memory before parsing with goquery. Proper resource cleanup was implemented with deferred Close calls for both the compression readers and the underlying response body, using a flag to track whether the body was wrapped. The brotli dependency was added to go.mod and go.sum to enable brotli decompression support.

Hey, I need to clean up how we handle .NET SDK updates in this repo. Right now we have this massive PowerShell script called update-dotnet-sdk.ps1 that does all the heavy lifting - it reads global.json, fetches release notes from the dotnet/core repo, compares versions, creates branches, commits changes, and even opens pull requests automatically. The whole thing is over 400 lines of code that we're maintaining ourselves.

The better approach here is to use a dedicated GitHub Action instead. There's already a reusable action available at martincostello/update-dotnet-sdk that does exactly what our script does, but it's maintained separately and we don't have to keep all that logic in our codebase. This means we can delete the PowerShell script entirely and just reference the action in our workflow file.

I also noticed the workflow is currently running on windows-latest, but since we're switching to a GitHub Action, we can run it on ubuntu-latest instead which is generally faster and cheaper. The job name should also be more descriptive than just "build" since this is specifically for SDK updates.

Plan: 1. Update .github/workflows/update-dotnet-sdk.yml to use the martincostello/update-dotnet-sdk@v1 action instead of running the PowerShell script directly, change the runner to ubuntu-latest, and rename the job to update-dotnet-sdk. 2. Delete update-dotnet-sdk.ps1 entirely since the action handles all that logic now.

